# Expanding limits: expand_limits

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:giggly, path: Path.join(__DIR__, "..")},
  {:kino, "~> 0.19"}
  ])
```

## About this notebook

This notebook follows the examples on ggplot2's reference page for
[`expand_limits()`](https://ggplot2.tidyverse.org/reference/expand_limits.html), in the order the
page gives them. Each example shows ggplot2's code first and then Giggly's. An example Giggly
cannot draw yet keeps its ggplot2 code and names what it is waiting for, so the page is also a list
of what remains.

`expand_limits()` widens a plot's scales to include the values it is given, in every panel, by
adding a layer that draws nothing at those values (a `geom_blank()`). ggplot2 now prefers giving a
scale's `limits` a function instead, such as `scale_x_continuous(limits = ~range(.x, 0))` to
include zero. Giggly has none of this yet: it has no scale functions, and a position scale is
chosen from the data and cannot be set.

## Including a value on an axis

```r
p <- ggplot(mtcars, aes(mpg, wt)) + geom_point()
p + expand_limits(x = 0)
p + expand_limits(y = c(1, 9))
p + expand_limits(x = 0, y = 0)
```

Not in Giggly yet: `expand_limits()`.

## Widening a colour scale

The first plot widens a continuous colour scale to run from 2 to 10; the second adds 2, 4, 6, 8
and 10 to a discrete one, so its legend lists them all, though no car has 2 or 10 cylinders.

```r
ggplot(mtcars, aes(mpg, wt)) +
  geom_point(aes(colour = cyl)) +
  expand_limits(colour = seq(2, 10, by = 2))
ggplot(mtcars, aes(mpg, wt)) +
  geom_point(aes(colour = factor(cyl))) +
  expand_limits(colour = factor(seq(2, 10, by = 2)))
```

Not in Giggly yet: `expand_limits()`, mapping `colour` and the legend that reads it back, and an
expression in a mapping, such as `factor(cyl)`.
